[0m[32mI respectfully disagree. While environmental stewardship is important, a carbon tax isn't the right solution and raises several concerns. First, it amounts to a regressive tax that disproportionately burdens working families and the poor, who spend a larger percentage of their income on energy and goods. Second, unilateral U.S. carbon taxes disadvantage our economy while countries like China and India continue expanding energy production without similar constraints. Third, free markets and innovation—not government mandates—have driven remarkable improvements in air and water quality over decades. We've cut emissions significantly while growing our economy. Rather than taxing carbon, we should encourage market-driven innovation in energy technology, invest in nuclear power, and remove regulatory barriers to cleaner energy development. We should also trust American ingenuity and capitalism to solve these problems more efficiently than government bureaucrats. The Constitution gives us the freedom and responsibility to make our own choices about consumption and investment. A carbon tax expands government control over the economy in ways that undermine personal liberty without proven climate benefits, especially when other nations don't share the burden.<|eot_id|>[0m
