[0m[32mI respectfully disagree with that premise. History shows that companies innovate most when they can retain profits and invest in R&D without excessive government intervention. Breaking up successful tech companies would actually discourage innovation by punishing success and creating regulatory uncertainty. Instead of antitrust action, we should focus on removing barriers to entry for competitors. The real problem isn't that big tech companies exist—it's that government regulations often protect incumbents and make it too expensive for startups to compete. If we truly want more innovation, we should cut red tape, lower compliance costs, and let the free market work. Companies that fail to innovate will naturally lose market share to competitors. We don't need government picking winners and losers through forced breakups.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
