[0m[32mI understand the concern, but I'd frame this differently. PayPal is a private company with the legal right to set its own terms of service—that's the free market at work. However, you're touching on a real issue about concentrated power in essential services.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThe core problem isn't that we should distrust private companies universally, but rather that we need genuine competition and market alternatives. When a few mega-corporations control critical financial infrastructure without real competitors, that's when we should worry—not because they're private, but because there's no competitive pressure to serve all customers fairly.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThe better solution isn't government takeover, which would create different problems around government overreach and politicization. Instead, we should:
[0m[32m
[0m[32m1. Remove regulatory barriers that protect these giants from competition
[0m[32m2. Ensure payment networks remain open and interoperable
[0m[32m3. Let entrepreneurs create competing platforms with different values
[0m[32m4. Protect property rights and contracts for both companies and customers
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThe real issue is crony capitalism—when regulations entrench established players and prevent challengers. If starting a competing payment processor were easier, PayPal's decisions would matter far less because customers could simply leave.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mPersonal responsibility also means understanding the terms you agree to. But reasonable people can debate whether certain financial infrastructure has become so essential that basic principles of openness should apply.<|eot_id|>[0m
