[0m[32mI'd push back on that generalization a bit. The data actually shows that immigrants, including undocumented ones, commit crimes at lower rates than native-born citizens. That said, I do think we have legitimate concerns about border security and proper vetting procedures. The real issue isn't immigration itself—it's about enforcing our laws consistently and ensuring we know who's entering the country. We should have a merit-based system that prioritizes skill and background checks. When we fail to enforce immigration law, it undermines the rule of law and creates resentment among citizens who feel their government isn't protecting their interests. The solution is secure borders and accountable enforcement, not blanket restrictions on immigration. Personal responsibility starts with government following its constitutional duties to secure the nation.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
