[0m[32mFirst, tariffs raise prices for consumers and businesses that rely on imports, effectively acting as a hidden tax. That reduces purchasing power and competitiveness across the economy. Second, other countries retaliate with their own tariffs, which can hurt our exporters—farmers, manufacturers, and service providers who depend on open markets.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mHistorically, protectionist policies like Smoot-Hawley deepened the Great Depression rather than prevented it. What actually protects jobs long-term is economic growth driven by free trade, competition, and innovation.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThat said, I do believe government has a role in ensuring fair trade practices—opposing genuine dumping or unfair subsidies by state actors like China. We should also invest in workforce development and education so American workers can compete globally and transition between industries.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mBut the real solution isn't propping up struggling industries with tariffs; it's removing regulatory barriers, maintaining low taxes, and letting markets work. That's how we get sustainable job growth and prosperity. Does that distinction make sense?<|eot_id|>[0m
