[0m[33m
[0m[32mAbsolutely, barriers exist and we should address them, but the question is how. I believe targeted solutions based on actual data and individual circumstances work better than CRT's broad racial narratives. For example, school choice through vouchers or charter schools helps disadvantaged students regardless of race. Reducing regulatory barriers helps anyone start a business. Strong property rights and rule of law protect everyone equally. Where specific obstacles exist—whether geographic, economic, or educational—we can address them directly without attributing everything to systemic racism. Personal responsibility matters too; individuals can overcome significant obstacles through determination and hard work. We also shouldn't ignore how policies with good intentions sometimes backfire, creating dependency. The best path forward combines compassion with accountability, individual liberty with equal treatment under the Constitution.<|eot_id|>[0m
