[0m[33mBut wouldn't that create inconsistency in what students learn? How would schools manage teaching if some students skip certain lessons?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's a fair logistical question. Schools already manage different learning levels and specialized programs, so handling opt-outs is administratively feasible. The key is that schools should offer alternatives—maybe independent study, different classroom assignments, or supplemental materials that align with parental preferences. We do this in many contexts already without chaos. More importantly, some inconsistency is actually preferable to forcing uniformity. Not every student needs identical curriculum. What matters is students master fundamental skills—math, reading, critical thinking, and writing. Beyond that core, there's room for diverse approaches. We shouldn't sacrifice parental authority and family values just for administrative convenience. If schools truly believed in their curriculum, they should be able to articulate why it's necessary and persuade parents, rather than demanding compliance. When government mandates one-size-fits-all education, it inevitably conflicts with many families' beliefs and constitutional rights to direct their children's upbringing. Flexibility and choice ultimately strengthen education by making it more responsive to communities.<|eot_id|>[0m
