[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's the classic security-versus-liberty argument, and it's tempting to accept that trade-off on the surface. But consider this: once we surrender constitutional protections, we rarely get them back. And history shows surveillance powers get repurposed beyond their original intent—against political opponents, journalists, religious groups, and minorities. We've seen this with COINTELPRO and other programs. More fundamentally, if we abandon our constitutional principles to fight terrorism, haven't the terrorists already won by destroying what makes America worth defending? I believe we can be both secure and free. We should invest in targeted, warrant-based intelligence, strengthen cybersecurity infrastructure, and encourage the private sector's role in security—this respects both safety and constitutional rights. Personal responsibility and limited government power actually serve security better long-term than surveillance states, which breed distrust and inefficiency.<|eot_id|>[0m
