[0m[32mI respectfully disagree with that premise. Inheritance taxes, or death taxes as many call them, actually undermine several important principles. First, they violate the fundamental right of individuals to dispose of their own property as they see fit. People already paid taxes on that wealth when they earned it—taxing it again when they pass it to their children amounts to double taxation.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mSecond, these taxes are economically inefficient. They discourage saving, investment, and entrepreneurship. Family businesses and farms often have to be sold to pay estate taxes, destroying jobs and local economies. The compliance costs are enormous, and much of the revenue goes to tax attorneys and accountants rather than government coffers.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThird, inequality is better addressed through economic growth and opportunity, not wealth redistribution. When you expand free markets and reduce regulations, you create jobs and economic mobility that lift people up. High earners and successful businesses create employment and innovation.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mFinally, regarding public services—government spending isn't constrained by tax revenue in the way many assume. The real issue is wasteful spending and inefficient programs. We should focus on limited, constitutional government that does essential things well, not on raising more revenue through punitive taxation.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mPersonal responsibility and keeping what you've earned—including passing it to your family—are core to a free society.<|eot_id|>[0m
