[0m[32mI appreciate the concern, but I'd actually push back on this one. Look, I'm not saying social media companies should have zero responsibility—they absolutely should enforce their terms of service and remove illegal content promptly. But making them fully liable for user-generated content is a different animal entirely.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mHere's the conservative case: First, it's fundamentally about property rights. These are private companies with their own platforms. They should have the freedom to set their own editorial policies without heavy-handed government mandates. Second, if we impose full liability, we're essentially forcing them to pre-screen every single post—which either means massive censorship of speech or they'll just shut down entirely. That's counterproductive.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mThird, Section 230 of the Communications Decency Act exists for a reason. Without it, we wouldn't have had the internet ecosystem we have. The better approach is to hold platforms accountable through market forces and consumer choice—if they're not moderating responsibly, users can migrate to alternatives. We're already seeing that happen.
[0m[32m
[0m[32mWhat we should focus on instead is going after the actual bad actors—the ones posting illegal content directly—and making sure platforms respond when law enforcement puts pressure on them. But blanket corporate liability? That's big government overreach dressed up as consumer protection.<|eot_id|>[0m
