[0m[32mThat's a fair question about market externalities. However, there are better approaches than government redistribution of subsidies. If there are genuine environmental concerns, we can address them through property rights and tort law—if someone's pollution damages your property, you have legal recourse. That creates accountability without expanding government control. We should also be skeptical of the premise that government picking energy winners is the answer. Renewable subsidies often benefit wealthy corporations and special interests, not consumers. Look at how solar and wind subsidies concentrate benefits among well-connected companies. A more principled conservative approach is to reduce regulations that unnecessarily slow development of all energy sources, invest in basic research through competitive grants, and let entrepreneurs innovate. American innovation has historically solved problems more effectively than government mandates. We can address environmental concerns while respecting economic freedom and letting markets work. And frankly, American natural gas has already reduced emissions more than many renewable policies—that's the free market working.<|eot_id|>[0m
