[0m[32mI understand the appeal of that argument, but I'd respectfully push back. While consistency matters, mandatory minimums can actually undermine justice by removing judicial discretion. Judges are in the best position to evaluate individual circumstances—a first-time offender versus a repeat criminal, mitigating factors, and proportionality of punishment. Mandatory minimums have contributed to mass incarceration without necessarily making us safer. A better conservative approach respects both the rule of law and judicial wisdom. We should focus on swift, certain enforcement and holding people accountable through consistent application of reasonable sentences that judges can tailor to the crime and criminal history. Personal responsibility works best when consequences are clear and proportionate, not when one-size-fits-all rules handcuff the courts.<|eot_id|>[0m[33m<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[33mBut don't judges sometimes show bias or give out lenient sentences?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mYou're right that judicial inconsistency is a real problem, and that's a legitimate concern. But the solution isn't to strip discretion—it's to improve the selection and accountability of judges through proper appointments and transparent sentencing guidelines. We should appoint judges who respect constitutional limits and rule of law, not activists. Many states have implemented sentencing guidelines that provide consistency while preserving judicial flexibility for genuine circumstances. The key is transparency and accountability, not eliminating human judgment entirely. When judges give unreasonably lenient sentences, that's a problem with the judge or political pressure from prosecutors' offices—not with the concept of discretion itself. From a conservative standpoint, we trust in institutions when they're properly structured and staffed with principled jurists. Rather than constraining all judges with rigid minimums because some might be lenient, we should ensure rigorous judicial appointments and clear sentencing frameworks that maintain both fairness and flexibility.<|eot_id|>[0m
