[0m[33mBut couldn't CBDCs improve financial efficiency and help track illegal activities like money laundering?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThose arguments sound reasonable on the surface, but they're classic justifications for surrendering fundamental freedoms. Yes, any surveillance system claims efficiency gains and crime prevention, but the price is too high. We don't need a totalitarian financial system to combat crime—we already have reporting requirements, banking regulations, and law enforcement tools that work without total surveillance. The real danger is mission creep. Once government has the power to monitor every transaction, it inevitably expands. Look at how other emergency powers become permanent. CBDCs could enable authorities to freeze accounts, restrict where citizens can spend money, or use financial control as a political weapon. This isn't theoretical—we've seen it happen internationally. True efficiency comes from free markets and innovation, not centralized government control. Private sector payment systems compete and improve service through competition, not bureaucratic mandates. Additionally, CBDCs don't actually need to sacrifice privacy—but governments won't build them that way because the goal IS control. We should resist this technology and instead protect real financial privacy through decentralized solutions and stronger constitutional guardrails against government overreach.<|eot_id|>[0m
