[0m[33mBut what about manufacturing jobs that have moved overseas? Shouldn't we protect those workers from foreign competition?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>
[0m[33m
[0m[32mThat's a fair concern, and those job losses are real hardships for real families. But we have to look at the actual data and think through the unintended consequences. When we impose tariffs or restrict trade to 'protect' manufacturing jobs, we're essentially taxing American consumers and businesses that use those materials. A steel tariff might save some steel jobs, but it costs jobs in automobile manufacturing, construction, and appliances because those industries face higher input costs. We end up losing more jobs than we save. The real path forward is fostering an environment where American manufacturers can thrive through innovation and efficiency, not through government protection. We should be cutting regulations that make manufacturing here unnecessarily expensive, investing in workforce development so workers can transition to growing sectors, and maintaining the rule of law and property rights that make America attractive for investment. When we trust free markets and personal responsibility rather than government central planning, workers have genuine opportunities. Countries that embraced protectionism—look at the results. We need to compete globally by being smarter and more efficient, not by hiding from competition.<|eot_id|>[0m
